meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
==========================

This layer contains the graphics-stack-independent RZ/G3L fast-boot work:

  Phase 1  Device-tree pruning and Linux kernel configuration trimming.
  Phase 2  Rootfs slimming and systemd startup-order optimisation.

It intentionally keeps the BSP's Weston graphics stack. Build
`core-image-weston` with the `rz-vlp-fast` distro to reproduce the phase-2
baseline. The separate `meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-qt-eglfs` layer adds phase 3 and
replaces Weston with Qt6/EGLFS.

Serial console silencing and bootdelay are U-Boot environment settings and are
not baked into either layer. See section V.


I. What this layer changes
==========================

  Distro (conf/distro/rz-vlp-fast.conf)
    - derives from rz-vlp and pins linux-renesas 6.12
    - removes unused DISTRO_FEATURES and MACHINE_FEATURES
    - removes the RZ/VLP development and benchmark package groups
    - disables autostart for services whose features were removed
    - fixes SERIAL_CONSOLES to use ttySC3

  Kernel (recipes-kernel/linux/)
    - the DT patch disables unused network, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio,
      extra UART, RTC and related peripheral nodes
    - fastboot.cfg removes unused kernel subsystems and drivers while keeping
      the display, boot storage and thermal-protection paths

  Userspace
    - fastboot-mask-units.bbclass masks vconsole setup and the invalid ttyS0
      getty during rootfs construction
    - systemd-conf_%.bbappend keeps journald in RAM and stops serial forwarding
    - weston-init.bbappend lets Weston start in parallel with multi-user.target


II. Required layers
===================

Use a standard RZ/G3L VLP project with at least:

  poky/meta
  poky/meta-poky
  poky/meta-yocto-bsp
  meta-openembedded/meta-oe
  meta-openembedded/meta-python
  meta-openembedded/meta-multimedia
  meta-arm/meta-arm-toolchain
  meta-arm/meta-arm
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-bsp
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-distro
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-codecs
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-graphics
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

This layer has priority 20 and must follow the Renesas BSP, distro and graphics
layers in bblayers.conf.

Tested with Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0-rc1 and linux-renesas
6.12.46-cip8.


III. Build the phase-2 Weston baseline
======================================

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

Set in conf/local.conf:

  MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l"
  DISTRO  = "rz-vlp-fast"

Then build:

  $ bitbake core-image-weston

Useful metadata checks:

  $ bitbake-layers show-layers
  $ bitbake-layers show-appends
  $ bitbake -e core-image-weston | \
        grep -E '^(DISTRO|PREFERRED_VERSION_linux-renesas|SERIAL_CONSOLES)='


IV. Add the Qt/EGLFS phase
==========================

Do not copy the base files into another layer. Add the overlay after this layer:

  meta-qt6
  meta-rz-qt6
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-qt-eglfs

Then select:

  DISTRO = "rz-vlp-fast-qt-eglfs"

and build:

  $ bitbake core-image-hmi


V. U-Boot environment
=====================

The stock bootcmd resets the environment before loading the image:

  bootcmd = env default -a; run bootcmd_check; run bootimage

Remove that reset before saving fast-boot settings:

  => setenv bootcmd 'run bootcmd_check; run bootimage'
  => setenv bootdelay 0
  => setenv prodemmcbootargs 'setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 quiet loglevel=4 systemd.show_status=0'
  => setenv prodsdbootargs  'setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk1p2 quiet loglevel=4 systemd.show_status=0'
  => saveenv
  => reset

Dropping earlycon avoids replaying the kernel ring buffer synchronously over
the 115200-baud UART. Re-add earlycon when debugging an early kernel failure.


VI. Product constraints
=======================

The DT profile disables Ethernet, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio, i2c0/i2c1/
i2c3, extra UARTs and RTC. Restore both the DT nodes and their matching kernel
and userspace features before adding any of those product functions.

The microSD controller stays enabled for the development boot loop. A fixed
eMMC product can disable it after manufacturing and recovery requirements are
settled.
